    "topic_id": 352090,
    "poster_id": 652,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/9/029573298dbfa5108225c9c8962fad9f4140479a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:\\mathbb N\\rightarrow\\mathbb N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" > be a non-decreasing function and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > be an arbitrary natural number. Suppose that there are prime numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/9/e39984255e8fd854c8bfe929d129b7e7013c3cc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1,p_2,\\dots,p_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"11\" > and natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/f/e9ffeec0f5d31561466dc54d2a5df685e51a28fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_1,s_2,\\dots,s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"11\" > such that for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/d/20dc85bf0e34f8d7fbf66b886eedf3bee98aca45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq i\\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" > the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/3/ca3bb3cecca61a529354fa514c3a41388a38201e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{f(p_ir+s_i)|r=1,2,\\dots\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"198\" height=\"18\" > is an infinite arithmetic progression. Prove that there is a natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"8\" > such that<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/0/5f0b90eb01448b80aa67e5d641a991b2e80349a7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(a+1), f(a+2), \\dots, f(a+n)\\]\"  width=\"246\" height=\"18\" ><br>\nform an arithmetic progression.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $f:\\mathbb N\\rightarrow\\mathbb N$ be a non-decreasing function and let $n$ be an arbitrary natural number. Suppose that there are prime numbers $p_1,p_2,\\dots,p_n$ and natural numbers $s_1,s_2,\\dots,s_n$ such that for each $1\\leq i\\leq n$ the set $\\{f(p_ir+s_i)|r=1,2,\\dots\\}$ is an infinite arithmetic progression. Prove that there is a natural number $a$ such that\n\\[f(a+1), f(a+2), \\dots, f(a+n)\\]\nform an arithmetic progression.",
    "username": "Omid Hatami",
